.         
.         *The list of control variables stored as a local named "control_varia
> bles"
.         
.         local control_variables male age years_of_education household_income 
> i.marital_status i.employment_status i.migrant

.         
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 *
. 
.         * Verbal description of the model:
.                 *Dependent variable: plh0303_modified: Fam: Men Involved in H
> ousework
.                 *Independent variable: has_daughter (created above)
.                 
.                 *In this analysis, we assume that "plh0303" is a reasonable p
> roxy of preferences toward gender equality in the household. We hypothesize t
> hat having a daughter
.                 *correlates with being more supportive of gender equality in 
> the household. In line with this hypothesis, we run a linear regression of th
> e "plm0644_modified"
.                 *dependent variable on the "has_daughter" independent variabl
> e. We use robust standard errors due to a potential relationship between the 
> variance of the dependent
.                 *and the binary variable controlling the preserence of daught
> ers in the household.
.                 *While we perform a causal hypothesis test and the main treat
> ment variable "has_daughters" should not be correlated with the remaining det
> erminants of the dependent variable
.                 *in the population, due to potential relationships arising be
> tween the treatment variable and other determinants as a result of (i) the sm
> all size of the sample, and (ii) the sample
.                 *selection procedure, we also control for the variables we be
> lieve to be among the primary determinants of the dependent variable.
. 
.         regress plh0303_modified has_daughter `control_variables', robust

